>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} lang="en" />
        <h1 class="hero__h1">Adultery and Divorce in the UAE</h1>
        <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated October 2026</p>
        <p class="hero__sub">
          An affair touches two separate areas of UAE law. In criminal law, Article 409 of Federal
          Decree-Law No. 31 of 2021 makes consensual intercourse outside marriage an offence, but only
          the spouse or a guardian can start a case. In family law, a divorce for harm, khula or, for
          non-Muslims, a no-fault civil divorce is what actually ends the marriage; our
          <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law guide</a> covers the statutes behind it.
        </p>
        <ul class="hero__trust">
          <li>✓ Muslim &amp; non-Muslim tracks</li>
          <li>✓ Article 409 complaint rule</li>
          <li>✓ Custody &amp; finance effects</li>
          <li>✓ Free legal consultation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__form">
        <LeadForm lang="en" variant="hero" heading="Free Divorce Consultation" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="container main-content">

    <!-- Intro / why the law matters -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
      <h2 id="intro-heading">Adultery and Divorce in the UAE: Start Here</h2>
      <p>
        Few things in a marriage are as painful as discovering infidelity, and in the UAE the
        subject sits across two very different areas of law at once: family law, which decides your
        divorce, custody and finances, and criminal law, which can treat the affair as an offence. The
        two do not always move together, and getting them confused can lead people into serious
        mistakes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rules have changed more than once. The criminal provisions were rewritten in 2020 and
        again in 2021, and many articles online still describe a stage that no longer applies, either
        claiming that people are automatically jailed or claiming that sex outside marriage was
        decriminalised. Neither matches the current text. The offence is in Article 409 of Federal
        Decree-Law No. 31 of 2021 on the Crimes and Penalties Law, and it can only be prosecuted on a
        complaint by the spouse or the guardian. This page sets out what the law says and what it
        means for a divorce.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box info-box--gold" style="margin-top:8px;">
        <h3>This is information, not legal advice</h3>
        <p>
          DivorceU.AE is a referral service, not a law firm. Criminal-law consequences in this area
          are fact-sensitive and can be severe. Nothing here should be treated as advice for your
          situation. Speak to a licensed UAE lawyer before you act, especially before confronting a
          spouse, gathering evidence or filing any complaint.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Legal action: criminal law -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="legal-action-heading">
      <h2 id="legal-action-heading">Can You Take Legal Action Against an Extramarital Affair in the UAE?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, but only in a narrow way, and it is not the same as getting divorced. The spouse of the
        person who had the affair, or a guardian, can file a criminal complaint under Article 409 of
        the Crimes and Penalties Law, and nobody else can start that case. A proven offence can lead
        to prison for both people involved, but the complaint does not end the marriage: that needs a
        separate family case under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 (the Muslim personal status law)
        or Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 (the civil law for non-Muslims).
      </p>

      <h3>What Article 409 says</h3>
      <p>
        Article 409(1) punishes a person aged 18 or over who has consensual sexual intercourse with a
        woman aged 18 or over with imprisonment of not less than six months, and the woman who
        consents faces the same penalty. Clause (1)(b) covers same-sex conduct between adults. The
        article is framed around age and consent, and it does not use the word “adultery”. What limits
        it in practice is the complaint rule in clause (5).
      </p>

      <h3>Only the spouse or guardian can complain, and can withdraw</h3>
      <p>
        Under Article 409(5), no criminal action can be brought for these offences except on a
        complaint by the spouse or the guardian. A relative, neighbour or employer cannot start the
        case. The spouse or guardian can also waive the complaint, and the waiver ends the criminal
        action or suspends the penalty. Practitioners stress that a waiver has to be clear and
        express: it is not presumed from a family settlement, so if you settle, say so in writing to
        the prosecution or court handling the case.
      </p>

      <h3>If a child is born: Article 410</h3>
      <p>
        Article 410 sets imprisonment of not less than two years for a man and a woman aged 18 or
        over whose relationship produced a child outside marriage. There is no case, or the case ends
        or the penalty is suspended, if the man marries the woman, or if either or both of them
        acknowledge the child’s paternity and the child is issued identity and travel documents.
      </p>

      <h3>Keep it off social media: Article 313(2)</h3>
      <p>
        Article 313(2) makes it an offence to publish news of investigations or proceedings in
        adultery cases, and in marriage, divorce, custody, alimony, separation and lineage cases. The
        penalty is imprisonment of up to two years or a fine. Posting about a spouse’s affair or about
        the case online can therefore create a criminal problem for the person posting. Our guide to
        <a href="/en/social-media-child-custody-uae/">social media and child custody</a> covers how
        posts can also surface in a custody dispute.
      </p>

      <h3>Criminal complaint or divorce claim: two different cases</h3>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Question</th>
              <th>Criminal complaint (Article 409)</th>
              <th>Family case (divorce)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Who can start it</td>
              <td>Only the spouse or the guardian (Article 409(5))</td>
              <td>A spouse, on the route open to them (talaq, khula, a court claim, or civil divorce)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Where it goes</td>
              <td>Police and Public Prosecution, then the criminal court</td>
              <td>The family court of the competent emirate (the Personal Status Court, or the court that hears civil family cases for non-Muslims)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>What it decides</td>
              <td>Whether the offence happened; imprisonment of not less than six months for both people</td>
              <td>Whether the marriage ends, plus custody, maintenance and other financial claims</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Does it end the marriage?</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Yes, by court judgment, khula or a documented talaq</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Can it be stopped?</td>
              <td>Yes: a waiver ends the action or suspends the penalty</td>
              <td>Not governed by Article 409; follows the family and civil procedure rules</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">Sources: Arts. 409 and 410 of Federal Decree-Law No. 31 of 2021; Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024; Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022.</p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The two are separate. A complaint does not by itself change your marital status, and you can
        bring a divorce claim without filing any complaint at all. If you do want a divorce, you
        still have to start the family case, and the family court decides it under its own law.
      </p>

      <h3>How an affair fits the divorce routes</h3>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Divorce for harm (Muslim track)</h3>
          <p>
            Article 71 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 deals with revocation of the marriage for
            damage, usually called divorce for harm. An affair can be put forward as part of the harm.
            If harm is disputed, the court appoints two arbitrators, who have up to 60 days to report
            (Article 72).
          </p>
          <a href="/en/divorce-for-harm-uae/" class="card-link">Divorce for harm guide</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Khula (Muslim track)</h3>
          <p>
            Under Articles 65 and 66 the wife can end the marriage for a consideration, which can be
            any property; if it is the dowry, returning what she received is enough. If the husband
            stubbornly refuses, the court orders khula for an appropriate consideration. No proof of
            the affair is needed, and children’s rights cannot be traded away.
          </p>
          <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/" class="card-link">Khula divorce guide</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Civil divorce (non-Muslims)</h3>
          <p>
            Under Article 7 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 either spouse’s wish to separate is
            enough. The affair is irrelevant to getting divorced, but Article 6 of Cabinet Resolution
            No. 122 of 2023 names infidelity as an example of fault the court may weigh for
            compensation.
          </p>
          <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/" class="card-link">Non-Muslim divorce guide</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Custody (both tracks)</h3>
          <p>
            Article 113 of the 2024 law sets conditions for a custodian, including being trustworthy
            and having no conviction for a sexual crime. An affair is not an automatic bar; the court
            decides on the child’s interest.
          </p>
          <a href="/en/child-custody-uae/" class="card-link">Child custody guide</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Two tracks -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="tracks-heading">
      <h2 id="tracks-heading">Two Legal Tracks, Two Different Answers</h2>
      <p>
        The UAE runs a dual family-law system, and adultery is treated very differently on each
        track. Which one applies to you depends on religion and, for non-Muslim foreigners, on whether
        one of you insists on the law of your home country.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Muslim (Sharia) track</h3>
          <p>
            Governed by Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, based on Islamic Sharia. An affair can be
            raised as harm to support a court divorce, and conduct can feed into custody and
            financial questions. See our
            <a href="/en/sharia-divorce-uae/">Sharia divorce guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Non-Muslim (civil) track</h3>
          <p>
            Governed by Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022. Divorce is no-fault, so you do not have to
            prove cheating to end the marriage. Adultery is largely irrelevant to the divorce itself
            but may surface in financial claims. See our
            <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Beneath both of these sits the criminal law, which applies whatever your family-law track
        and is explained in the section above. The point to carry forward: a criminal complaint and a
        divorce claim are separate cases with separate rules.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Adultery as a ground: harm / darar -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="darar-heading">
      <h2 id="darar-heading">Adultery as a Ground for Divorce: Harm (Darar)</h2>
      <p>
        On the Muslim track, the law does not list adultery as a standalone ground. A spouse who
        wants the court to end the marriage relies instead on Article 71 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41
        of 2024, revocation for damage, usually called divorce for harm (darar), and puts the affair
        forward as part of the harm. Whether it is enough is for the court on the evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters because it gives an exit route even where the other spouse refuses to agree to
        the divorce. The difference from khula is that khula is bought: the wife gives a
        consideration (Articles 65 and 66), while a harm claim asks the court to end the marriage
        because of the other spouse’s conduct. Under Article 102, a wife in a consummated marriage
        whose divorce or revocation was due to the husband can also claim compensation (mut’a) of up
        to one year’s maintenance. For the wider list of grounds a spouse can rely on, see our guides
        to <a href="/en/grounds-for-divorce-uae/">grounds for divorce in the UAE</a> and
        <a href="/en/divorce-for-harm-uae/">divorce for harm</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be realistic about it. Raising an affair as harm is not the same as proving it, and when harm
        is disputed the court can appoint two arbitrators, who have up to 60 days from their
        appointment to report (Article 72). Where the affair came with other conduct, such as
        desertion (Article 78) or failure to maintain (Article 77), those grounds have their own
        articles. Where the conduct also involves threats or abuse, our guide to
        <a href="/en/domestic-violence-divorce-uae/">domestic violence and divorce</a> covers
        protection under Federal Decree-Law No. 13 of 2024.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Evidence standard -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="evidence-heading">
      <h2 id="evidence-heading">The Evidence Problem: Why Adultery Is Hard to Prove</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest practical hurdle is proof. In the criminal case, the court has to be satisfied
        that the act happened, that it was consensual, and that the complaint came from someone
        entitled to make it. Practitioners writing on Article 409 cases describe courts that weigh the
        evidence as a whole rather than counting items, and that do not convict on suspicion.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>What can carry weight</h3>
          <p>
            A confession made before the Public Prosecution or the court; medical or forensic
            evidence, including DNA; a pregnancy or a child, where other circumstances support it;
            and messages or media that clearly show the act or are backed by other evidence.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Usually not enough on its own</h3>
          <p>
            Suspicion, accusations from family, the fact that two people were alone together, and
            messages that can be read more than one way. Screenshots or photos may still support a
            family-court harm claim, but on their own they rarely prove the criminal offence.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        There is a hard trade-off buried in this. Evidence gathered by recording someone without
        their consent, photographing them in private, or getting into their phone or accounts can
        itself be an offence under the UAE’s privacy and cybercrime laws, and it can expose you to a
        complaint of your own. This is exactly why do-it-yourself evidence gathering is risky, and why
        people are usually better off taking advice before they act rather than after.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Custody effect -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="custody-heading">
      <h2 id="custody-heading">Does Adultery Affect Child Custody?</h2>
      <p>
        Custody in the UAE is decided on the child’s interest, not as a reward or punishment for the
        parents’ behaviour in the marriage. Conduct matters through the conditions the law sets for a
        custodian. Under Article 113 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, a custodian must, among
        other things, be sane, aged 18 or over, trustworthy, free of addiction and of any conviction
        for a sexual crime, and, for a mother, not married to a man unrelated to the child unless the
        court decides otherwise. Custody lapses if a condition is lost (Article 115) and can be
        reclaimed when the reason ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        So an affair is not an automatic disqualification, and it is not a sure weapon either.
        Whether it shows that a parent is not trustworthy, and whether a particular conviction counts,
        is for the court. Custody now runs to 18 (Article 123), and from 15 the child may choose which
        parent to live with unless the child’s interest requires otherwise (Article 122). For
        non-Muslims, custody is joint and equal by default under Article 10 of Federal Decree-Law No.
        41 of 2022, and a parent can ask the court to remove the other for a reason it accepts. For
        how custody works in practice, see our
        <a href="/en/child-custody-uae/">child custody in the UAE guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Financial effect -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="finance-heading">
      <h2 id="finance-heading">Does Adultery Affect the Financial Settlement?</h2>
      <p>
        The link between infidelity and money is real but indirect, and it works differently on each
        track.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Muslim (Sharia) track</h3>
          <p>
            A wife in a consummated marriage who is divorced by her husband’s sole will, or whose
            divorce or revocation was due to him, can claim compensation (mut’a) of up to one year’s
            maintenance for women of her standing, set by his means, on top of iddah maintenance
            (Article 102). The dowry falls due on irrevocable separation in any case (Article 46). In
            khula the wife gives a consideration instead. See
            <a href="/en/wife-rights-after-divorce-uae/">wife rights after divorce</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Non-Muslim (civil) track</h3>
          <p>
            Under Article 9 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 the judge sets post-divorce alimony
            using factors that include the husband’s fault and harm, and Article 6 of Cabinet
            Resolution No. 122 of 2023 names fault such as infidelity or abandonment among the
            criteria for lump-sum compensation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        In neither system does adultery flip a switch that automatically hands one spouse the other’s
        assets. There is no automatic 50/50 split in the UAE, and the outcome turns on the factors
        the law lists, such as the length of the marriage, each spouse’s finances and any agreement,
        with conduct as one factor among several rather than the decisive one. Our
        <a href="/en/alimony-uae/">alimony in the UAE guide</a> explains how those claims are made.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Non-Muslim position -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="nonmuslim-heading">
      <h2 id="nonmuslim-heading">The Position for Non-Muslims: No-Fault by Design</h2>
      <p>
        For non-Muslim expats on the civil track, the point of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 is
        that either spouse can end the marriage without proving fault. Article 7 says one spouse’s
        wish to separate is enough, with no need to justify it, show harm or blame the other, and
        under Article 8 the court grants the divorce after the other spouse has been served. Divorce
        cases under this law are not referred to Family Guidance (Article 3). You do not have to prove
        your spouse cheated, and your spouse cannot block the divorce by denying it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where it can still matter is in the claims that follow: alimony, compensation and,
        indirectly, the atmosphere around custody. Under Article 1, a non-Muslim foreigner can also
        insist on the law of their home country, which may treat fault differently, so the choice of
        law can change how much infidelity counts. This is a strategic decision worth taking advice on
        early, and our <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">overview of divorce in the UAE</a> explains how the
        tracks fit together. The criminal rule in Article 409 is part of the general criminal
        law, not of either family-law track.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Practical advice -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="advice-heading">
      <h2 id="advice-heading">What to Do if You Suspect or Discover an Affair</h2>
      <p>
        The instinct to confront a cheating spouse, gather proof or report them is completely
        understandable. In the UAE, acting on that instinct without advice is where people get hurt,
        because the criminal layer can rebound on the accuser as well as the accused, and because
        badly gathered evidence can be worthless or unlawful. A calmer, sequenced approach protects
        you.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Get confidential legal advice first</h3>
          <p>
            Before you do anything else, speak to a licensed UAE family lawyer about your track, your
            realistic options and the risks of the criminal route. Most first consultations are free
            and confidential.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Do not gather evidence unlawfully</h3>
          <p>
            Recording someone without consent, photographing them in private, or getting into their
            phone or accounts can be offences under the UAE’s privacy and cybercrime laws. Let a
            lawyer tell you what evidence is both useful and lawful before you collect anything.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Do not post about it</h3>
          <p>
            Article 313(2) of the Crimes and Penalties Law makes publishing news of adultery or
            divorce proceedings an offence. Keep the affair and the case off social media.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Be careful what you say and admit</h3>
          <p>
            A confession before the prosecution or the court carries real weight. Do not admit to
            anything about your own conduct, and do not provoke a confrontation that could be turned
            against you, without advice first.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Treat the criminal complaint as a serious step</h3>
          <p>
            Only the spouse or a guardian can file it, and it can be waived later (Article 409(5)),
            but a conviction means imprisonment of not less than six months for both people, and it
            can affect custody, finances and both parties’ liberty. Weigh it with a lawyer, not in
            the heat of the moment.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
